WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.833
Start with one copy. One application serves event pages. Its safe measured capacity is finite, and a restart can interrupt every request reaching that copy.

2
00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:24.333
Add another copy. Deploy the same application on another machine or process. Horizontal scaling adds peers rather than increasing resources inside the original machine.

3
00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:35.500
Share incoming work. Routing sends requests to ready replicas. Balance according to workload characteristics; more boxes are useful only when requests can reach them.

4
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:47.250
Move durable state. Both replicas use the same appropriate storage for files, sessions or records. A file written only to Replica A will not magically appear on Replica B.

5
00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:58.417
Find serial work. If every request waits on one locked database operation, extra applications may add waiting instead of throughput. Trace and load-test the complete path.

6
00:00:58.417 --> 00:01:10.167
Test the gain. Compare throughput and tail latency before and after scaling. Also remove one replica to check that remaining copies serve users correctly with available capacity.
